      "generator": "Codex 内置 ImageGen",
      "prompt": "Use case: original atlas for six separate first-grade reading and picture-question illustrations. Exactly three columns by two rows of equal square tiles, clean fixed boundaries at thirds and half, no gutters, each full bleed and self contained. Essential objects stay at least 8 percent away from the edges and no overlap across tiles. Fine paper grain, matte gouache, clean cut-paper edges, deep pine green and warm cream, soft rain blue and apricot, persimmon orange accents, calm backdrops and easily recognizable objects. No text, letters, numbers, marks, arrows, grids or watercolor drips.\nTOP LEFT: a broad smooth empty ochre soil patch in a rain-blue landscape. Focus on a very simple blank open, slightly sunken expanse of dry sand with two cream stones in the far left corner of the foreground. The foreground must have absolutely no grass, flowers, weeds, tree or people, because flower and grass will be added by a separate layout later. The empty ground takes bottom 65 percent of tile. Gentle blue hills and sky far away in background only.\nTOP MIDDLE: a different simple landscape composition, a flat pale green meadow with a distinctive curved bank and one broad grey rock at the left edge. No detailed flowers or foreground grass blades at all: use uninterrupted plain pale green field in bottom 65 percent as a place to put separate grass/flower sprites later. Far-away blue tree silhouettes and warm pale sky in background only.\nTOP RIGHT: a distinct miniature cozy urban courtyard seen slightly from above. One low wide terracotta brick-edged rectangular soil bed dominates the lower two thirds. Dark bare soil in the bed is plain and empty, no leaves, grass, sprout, flowers or weeds. One small cream stone rests in the upper left corner of the bed. Muted warm flat wall and wooden gate behind. Entire planter visible and unchanged canvas framing for a later illustration layer.\nBOTTOM LEFT: new child with pine vest and orange cap at a low cream table in a calm classroom, visibly counting three bright teal-and-apricot round beads arranged in a line. One extended index finger points at the middle bead, the other hand shows fingers to count. No more beads or other things scattered on the table; make it obvious from thumbnail that the child is counting rather than moving an object.\nBOTTOM MIDDLE: the same orange-capped child on a similar cream table washes three clearly separate colorful round pebbles under a little trickle of water from a pitcher, both open hands low over a blue basin, no tally or pointing gesture. Show all the pebbles in the blue basin. This is a distinct action from counting.\nBOTTOM RIGHT: same orange-capped child holding two ends of one long horizontal clean apricot ribbon, with a second much shorter green ribbon lying flat on the cream table below. The child is clearly stretching out the long piece to see its length. No numbers, tick marks or ruler, simple composition with two distinct lengths clearly visible.\nNo typography or education poster. Original composition, no known textbook artwork. Keep simple bold shapes and objects visible in a 90 pixel thumbnail."
